From 20be0fb501761f48d484d5ccae0a1c1b19fcd3b2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1aeo <github@1aeo.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 10:12:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 12/13] dos: Add an off-by-default limit on open BEGIN_DIR
 streams per circuit

BEGIN_DIR returns before the stream creation defense, as the comment
there notes, so nothing bounds the BEGIN_DIR streams one circuit can
hold open (tor#41339). Each one costs a linked exit and directory
connection pair, which no connection limit counts and which can stay
open for minutes.

Add DoSBeginDirLimitEnabled, an AUTOBOOL whose "auto" default follows
the consensus parameter of the same name (default 0), and
DoSBeginDirMaxStreams (default 64). When enabled, a public relay
refuses a BEGIN_DIR stream on a circuit that already has that many open
directory streams. It sends END_STREAM_REASON_MISC, like the stream
defense, but tor clients ignore the reason on BEGIN_DIR streams: they
treat the refusal as a failed directory request and, except for onion
service requests, consider the relay down until their next consensus.

Stock clients rarely have more than a few directory streams open on one
circuit. While bootstrapping, a tor client launches about one
descriptor request per 500 relays, spread over up to three directory
guards, all on one circuit per guard; a bridge fetches both router
descriptors and microdescriptors, about 36 requests in a network of
9,000 relays. The default leaves room for that through a single guard.
BEGIN_DIR cells over the limit are counted as
tor_relay_dos_total{type="begindir_over_max"} whether or not the limit
is enabled, so operators can check that before enabling it.

Unlike reusing DoSStreamCreation*, this leaves exit streams and the
existing options and counters unchanged.

Part of #41339.

